  "slug": "as-of-july-2026-financial-and-bankruptcy-status-of-6c2b2dbf",
  "query": "As of July 2026, financial and bankruptcy status of these companies: Chegg Inc, America's Car-Mart Inc, JetBlue Airways, Peloton Inc, Faraday Future Intelligent Electric, Petco Health and Wellness. Which are at highest risk of bankruptcy filing in mid-2026? Any going concern warnings, debt maturities, or restructuring news?",
  "answer": "I've completed the research. Here's the summary of findings on the six companies' bankruptcy risk as of July 15, 2026:\n\n## Risk Ranking\n\n**HIGHEST RISK (mid-2026 filing risk, active going-concern warnings):**\n1. **America's Car-Mart (CRMT)** — Explicit going-concern warning; sought a $500M rescue loan via Houlihan Lokey to avoid default; lenders granted forbearance only until ~early September 2026, with asset sales/restructuring/bankruptcy explicitly on the table. FY2026 loss of $16.79/share, credit-loss provision $119.1M, net charge-offs 27.6%, stores cut from 154 to 94.\n2. **Faraday Future (FFAI)** — Going-concern doubt; company explicitly warns failed financings could lead it to seek Bankruptcy Code protection. Essentially pre-revenue ($512K Q1), ~$100M/yr burn, 0.43 current ratio, ~$0.16 stock.\n\n**ELEVATED but not imminent:**\n3. **Chegg (CHGG)** — No going-concern warning; AI has gutted revenue (~48% YoY decline) but it stayed standalone after a Goldman-led review, cut 45% of staff, and retains a net-cash cushion.\n4. **JetBlue (JBLU)** — $2.4B liquidity and ~$6.5B unencumbered assets, but ~$755-800M of 2026 maturities and mounting losses; some analysts flag high *2027* bankruptcy odds (multi-year thesis).\n\n**LOWER RISK (turnarounds progressing):**\n5. **Peloton (PTON)** — Back to profitability, repaid $199M of 2026 notes in cash, net debt down 70% to $173M, $1.13B cash.\n6. **Petco (WOOF)** — Roughly breakeven, positive EBITDA (~$75M/quarter), levered but serviceable; no going-concern warning.\n\n**Bottom line:** America's Car-Mart and Faraday Future are the two clearly at highest risk of a bankruptcy filing in mid-to-late 2026, both carrying explicit going-concern language and near-term liquidity cliffs.\n\nNote: The AskNews live-news tool returned a subscription error, so I relied on web search and SEC/press-release sources for the qualitative news; the quantitative financials came directly from the financial data tools. Full structured findings with data points and sources have been recorded.",
